● Merged. One app now — skysheet covers both projects and skills, sidebar gone.

  How it works now

  Whole sky → nine constellations, each project's name set dead centre of its
  own ring. Hovering a name shows its one-liner in the status bar; clicking it
  opens the project.

  Project open → the camera flies in and offsets the constellation, so the
  description sets in the top-left corner and the ring of skills sits in the
  space left over. The sky fades out under the text on a four-step ramp — a hard
  edge would read as a box, and the whole point is that there is no panel.

  Skill open → same frame, the description swaps for that skill's story, and its
  own star lights cyan on the ring so you can see which one you're reading.

  esc backs out one layer at a time: query → skill → project → sky.

  What the merge forced

  Your sketch changed the geometry, not just the layout:

  - Rings. The layout springs now have a rest length, so a project is a ring
  rather than a blob. It's an ellipse (~42×25) because a terminal and a
  paragraph are both far wider than they are tall — a circle sized to clear text
  horizontally is twice as tall as the frame.
  - Only exclusive skills get ring slots. Dealing a slot to a shared skill
  leaves a gap where it gets dragged away from, and the ring comes out as an arc
  with bites missing. Those seed at their own centre of gravity and end up
  outside the figure with a line still reaching back.
  - Names became the click target. A star is two subpixels across and its label
  is fifteen cells; asking someone to hit the star is asking them to hit the
  wrong thing.
  - The camera is placed by the typography. It picks whichever arm of the
  L-shaped free space is bigger — beside the text on a wide terminal, below it
  when the block is short, overlapping (pushed down-right) when neither fits.

  Three real bugs surfaced on the way: walking to a skill could select a star
  that was off-frame (nothing lit up — now the camera nudges the minimum amount
  after framing); the framing never updated when the text block changed shape
  between a 16-row project and a 7-row story; and the per-skill year field said
  "2026" 59 times, so it's gone.

  Also dropped lang — stats already began with the languages, and two fields
  saying the same thing will eventually disagree.

  45 tests, clippy clean, verified key-by-key through a real pty at 130×38 and
  82×24.

