● Tested it properly, and the data says the opposite of what we both expected —
  worth showing:

  Blocks-only glyph search (row 1) is a big jump. Adding quadrants and
  eighth-blocks to the search gives sub-cell precision with solid fill: Postgres
  finally reads as an elephant, Go's wordmark is clean, Rust's gear is sharp.
  Even at 12×6 cells (row 3) almost everything survives.

  Adding braille (rows 2 and 4) makes it worse. Look at Rust's outline, Tux's
  belly, Postgres's edges — braille gets selected at the boundaries and leaves
  dotted fringes on what should be solid edges. It's the same thing the map's
  README already worked out from the other side: "braille is dotted by
  construction — a braille road always reads as a dotted trail rather than a
  line." Braille wins for texture; it loses for marks, because it physically
  cannot fill a cell.

  So the answer to the blur is the glyph search, not braille:

  - Supersample → area-average → per-cell least-squares fit across space, █,
  half-blocks, all ten quadrants, and both eighth-block ramps.
  - Braille stays in the app for what it's good at (the map's terrain, any
  texture), and stays out of the logos.
  - 16×8 cells for the carousel strip and hero, 12×6 for a dense grid — both
  hold up.

  It also stays cheap at runtime: the script bakes glyph + fg alpha + bg alpha
  per cell, not baked colours, so the app can still dim a tile that a project
  doesn't use and shade one that's lifting under the cursor. That was the whole
  reason to avoid chafa's baked ANSI, and it survives.

