  Three things had to be true together or it reads as icons brightening rather
  than paper bending:

  - The sheet is drawn, not implied. The mesh threads are displaced by the same
  field and sampled along their length — a straight line between two lifted ends
  is a tent, not a bulge. First render had them nearly invisible and the effect
  didn't land at all; that was the fix that made it work.
  - Threads brighten where the sheet is steep. A fold catches light, a flat
  expanse doesn't. That gradient is most of what sells the surface.
  - Paint order is by lattice row, not screen row. A lifted tile is still at the
  same depth — it just rises. Sorting by screen position would push it behind
  the row in front of it, exactly backwards.

  The bump is round in visual units, not cell units — a cell is twice as tall as
  it is wide, so the falloff doubles vertical distance. Same class of bug as
  the logo aspect one, and there's a test pinning it.

  On the SSH cost you'd flagged: a still sheet redraws nothing and blocks on
  input indefinitely. Only a drifting sheet has a clock, at ~11 fps, and space
  stops it. Idle costs zero.

