  What changed

  Tiles now use the large art (16×8 cells instead of 10×5) with the pitch
  widened to match, and the mesh is gone. Losing the mesh costs the bend some
  legibility — that mesh was what made it read as paper rather than icons — but
  at this tile size the displacement is plain enough in the break it makes in
  the lattice's own rows, and the marks get the whole frame instead of sitting
  under a net.

  Motion is now velocity-integrated rather than position-derived:

  ┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              │                                                          │
  ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ resting      │ eases back to a steady drift, so it's always gently      │
  │ slide        │ moving                                                   │
  ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ wheel        │ a shove that adds — repeated scrolls build speed         │
  ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ drag         │ sheet nails to the pointer, and letting go hands the     │
  │              │ motion over                                              │
  ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ space        │ eases to a stop rather than freezing                     │
  └──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Decay is exponential, so a throw sheds most of its speed early then coasts.
  One real bug the tests caught: exponential decay never actually arrives, so a
  "held" sheet crept a fraction of a cell forever and never stopped asking to be
  redrawn. Velocity now snaps to rest once it's close enough.

  A finding you should see, because it changes a decision

  I measured what the drift costs on the wire:

  ┌────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐
  │          slide speed           │ bytes to the terminal │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ 1.6 cells/s (what I first set) │ 134 KB/s              │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ 0.8 cells/s                    │ 55 KB/s               │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ 0.35 cells/s                   │ 24 KB/s               │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
  │ held (space)                   │ 0.2 KB/s              │
  └────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘

  The cost is flat across frame rate — I tried 5, 7, 11 and 17 fps and got the
  same number. It isn't frame-bound; it's bound by cells changed per second,
  which is a function of how far the sheet travels, not how often it's drawn.
  Slowing the slide is the only lever, and it's linear.

  I've set the resting slide to 0.8 cells/s (~55 KB/s) as a middle position, but
  that's still ~450 kbit/s of continuous traffic for ambient motion on a
  portfolio you plan to serve over SSH. Three ways to go, and it's your call:

  1. Leave it. Fine on a LAN, unpleasant on hotel wifi.
  2. Slow it further to ~0.35 — 24 KB/s, still visibly alive.
  3. Default it off, so idle costs nothing and the sheet only moves when someone
  throws it. Space already toggles this; it's a one-line change to which way it
  starts.

  I'd pick 3 — the motion is most compelling as a response to input anyway, and
  an idle session costing zero is the right default for something strangers
  connect to.

