  How they're made. Each mark is drawn at exactly the resolution it's shown —
  one authored pixel per half-block — so nothing is resampled and nothing needs
  sharpening. Depth is a stack, not a projection: the face is drawn over four
  copies of itself stepped down-right, each darker than the one in front. Two
  details carry it — the step is one pixel on each axis, which is 45° on screen
  because a half-block pixel is square, and the face keeps a lit rim along its
  top-left, which is the only thing saying where the light is.

  The mistake worth recording: I first typed the shapes out as ASCII grids by
  hand, and every one came out as something other than intended — netjail read
  as a pill, gitswitch as noise. Geometry isn't a thing to eyeball a character
  at a time. They're now composed from a tiny shape language (disc, ring, rrect,
  tri, shield, union, sub), which makes them correct by construction and
  adjustable — a radius is a number rather than forty lines to retype.

  Two shapes needed a second pass after that: the toggle I drew for gitswitch
  had its knob fuse with its track into one lozenge, and vcs's commit nodes
  touched and read as a single blob. gitswitch became a key — a hole, a shaft
  and teeth are three features at three scales, so it survives coarse rendering,
  and it's nearer the truth since what gitswitch swaps is which SSH key signs
  your commits.

  The other half of this turn is more valuable than the marks. Your repos are
  public, so I pulled the real material — including netjail's 16 KB design doc.
  That gives me your actual engineering, not my inference:

  - Five named containment layers, L1–L5, two inside the namespace and three on
  the host — "which is what makes deleting the namespace firewall insufficient."
  - netjail run refuses to launch unless containment verifies, with no override 
  flag — and the preflight doesn't read rules, it makes a real connection
  attempt from inside and requires it to fail.
  - The logging guarantee is structural, not a matter of care: internal/events
  imports nothing from net/http, so no type in it can hold a header, a body or a
  raw URL.

