  Content came from experience/exp.md (I found it rather than inventing
  anything) into data/places.txt: five stops, oldest first, each with
  coordinates, a camera, and a sentence about what happened there.

  The flight

  The obvious implementation — interpolate centre and zoom independently — is
  unwatchable at street zoom: the middle of every journey is a grey blur and the
  ends crawl, and you arrive with no idea where you came from. So the camera
  flies the Van Wijk–Nuij arc (Smooth and Efficient Zooming and Panning, InfoVis
  2003), which holds perceived speed constant in screen-widths per second.
  Closed form, ~30 lines, no iteration.

  The altitude at the top isn't a number I picked — it falls out of the
  derivation as the height where both endpoints frame comfortably. Which means
  the Ahmedabad→Kapadwanj flight shows you the 60 km between them on the way
  past, with a marker on every stop:

  FLAT  z9.6  tilt 0°
    ◇ ◇ ◇ Ahmedabad ──── highway ──── Kapadvanj ◈

  I verified that frame, not just the maths. Two tests hold it: one
  differentiates the solution and checks the invariant ρ²(du/w)² + (dw/ρw)² = 1
  (holds to 1.02×), the other requires the arc to beat naive interpolation on
  peak pan rate by 6×.

  Travel is flat, arrival is 2.5D — the camera levels out in the first third,
  crosses as a plain map, and only tilts up once stopped. Doing both at once
  makes the tilt read as wobble. The caption fades out on the place you're
  leaving, vanishes mid-flight, and fades back in on arrival; under it the map
  dissolves to nothing over four rows rather than sitting in a bordered box.

  --place ID --from ID --at SECONDS renders any frame of any flight
  deterministically, which is how I checked all of the above.

  Two things worth your attention

  I set the tour zooms to ~14.4, not street level. The basemap has no building
  footprints outside Mumbai, so z16 over Ahmedabad is six roads on an empty
  plate — accurate and nothing to look at. At 14.4 the road network and
  neighbourhood labels arrive together and it reads as a place. Verified by
  comparison, not assumed.

  Bandwidth, since this is served over SSH. I measured it: idle 0.5 KB/s, but a
  flight was 1.7 MB/s. I cut that to ~1.0 MB/s by quantising the brightness ramp
  to 24 steps and emitting palette greys instead of truecolor for neutral cells
  (xterm's 232–255 is a 24-step grey ramp) — no visible change, I compared
  renders. It won't go much lower: a full-screen dithered map is a full-screen
  texture, and at ~11 bytes/cell a frame is ~100 KB. A flight is a 2–3 s burst,
  so ~2.5 MB per keypress, and a slow link plays it late rather than skipping.
  If that's too much for your deployment, the lever is flight frame rate — tell 
  me and I'll add a cap.

  One bug worth naming: the palette-index optimisation silently broke the fade,
  because toward_bg only understood Color::Rgb and skipped every indexed cell —
  the band cleared nothing while still appearing to work on the coloured parts.
  Fixed, and both regression tests were checked by reintroducing the bug and
  confirming they fail.

