  A scrolling long-form essay, not another fixed frame — the other sections
  animate in place, and a fourth of those would have made this a card wall.
  Emblems alternate down the spread, with momentum scrolling and snap-to-rest.

  On the images: I couldn't fetch any. Every image host is blocked from here
  except GitHub — Wikimedia, TMDB, Wikia, MAL, imgur all fail — and there's no
  character art on the machine.

  So I drew them instead, and I think it's the better answer regardless. A
  dithered 40-column crop of a copyrighted frame is a bad thing to put on a
  public server, and the object a character carries says more than a low-res 
  picture of their face: Snufkin is a hat, Iroh is a cup of tea, The Bear is a
  knife, One Piece is a straw hat. Twelve emblems composed from shape functions
  — flat with a lit rim, so they read as woodcuts.

  I rendered a contact sheet and fixed what didn't read: the hat was a mountain
  until the crown became a dome, the knife was a grey slab, then a rolling pin,
  before the blade got deep enough. A test asserts every entry names a drawing
  that exists — it caught two that didn't.

  If you'd rather have real stills, drop images anywhere I can reach and I'll
  build the photo pipeline; it's the same half-block technique the logos already
  use.

  ask

  Deliberately not a chat client. Questions set as marginalia in the accent,
  answers as prose in the essay's measure, and the wait is a tide — contour
  lines on the same braille canvas the map is made of, drifting while the agent
  thinks, still when it answers. A pure function of a clock, like everything
  else here.

  The security posture is the design, not a wrapper round it: the session is
  pinned to plan mode and refuses to continue if that call fails, every
  permission request is declined, and turns are capped at 12. Nothing is granted
  because nothing is needed — the whole portfolio is assembled into the first
  prompt from the same sheets the app renders, so the agent has no reason to
  reach for a tool.

  That needed JSON and serde isn't in the offline registry, so there's a small
  parser, with surrogate pairs and trailing-garbage rejection handled rather
  than assumed.

  One honest limit. I verified the handshake, plan mode, the prompt and the
  error path against a live opencode acp — but the provider host is blocked from
  this machine (Forbidden: host_not_whitelisted), so I have never seen a 
  streamed answer arrive end to end. The stream handling is tested against
  recorded message shapes. It should work where opencode has network; that's the
  first thing to try.

  Two things worth your attention before this goes anywhere public: the chat 
  spends your LLM credits on strangers' questions, and 12 turns per connection
  is the only brake — say the word and I'll add a global daily cap. And the
  taste essay is written in my voice from your notes; it's
  portfolio/data/taste.txt, read from disk, so edits need no rebuild.

