  The mark is three stacked shipping containers. The door ridges matter —
  without them it's three rectangles; with them it's the thing the name is
  about.

  The diagram is two columns, because harbr has two independent halves:

  - ONE DOOR — the key you connected with is the login; an unknown key files an
  access request, and an admin approving it lets the person in without
  reconnecting. Plus the first-key-claims-the-instance rule.
  - ONE DEPLOY — compose parsed in-process and driven straight at the Docker
  Engine API, BuildKit, then the services walking up through their health checks
  in dependency order, one at a time on a loop. That animation is the point: a
  service that never goes healthy fails the deploy rather than reporting success
  over a crash-looping container. Rollback re-runs recorded image ids, not
  tags.

  The footnote is the best detail in that repo: bind mounts resolve to absolute
  paths the Docker daemon reads on the host, so harbr's own data directory has
  to sit at the same path inside its container — /var/lib/harbr at
  /var/lib/harbr is load-bearing, not tidiness.

  Two tests earned their keep. I declared the footprint by eye at 78×26 and the
